[SLICE]
UID: SLICE-CHECKPOINT-WIRING
TITLE: Wire the behaviour model into the tool and the checkpoints
DEPTH: needs-design
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Add the lifecycle verbs to the tool: a move is a command that selects one
transition, checks its gates, and either refuses with a diagnostic or writes.
Register the existing checks -- fingerprint drift, cycles, plan boundaries,
the authorship guard -- as validators at the checkpoints the model derives for
them: the command before and after its write, the pre-commit hook against the
previous commit, CI against the point where the branch left main, and
strictdoc's own read-time and after-parse hooks as backstops for edits that
bypass the tool. Surface every gate that has no validator, or no checkpoint
able to see what it needs; whether that fails the evaluation or only reports
is settled at review. One such gate is known already: the authorship guard
needs both git history and who is acting, and no checkpoint sees both, so it
is recorded as unenforceable until agents have their own identity (milestone
five).

Consumer surface the operator reviews before implementation: the checkpoint
table -- which check runs where, and what is still unenforceable. Reviewed
after: a rule that used to live in the skill being refused by a running check.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
This is the milestone INV-GUARDS-MUST-EXECUTE asks for. Measured: strictdoc's
after-parse hook never runs when a node is created, so it is a backstop; its
read-time hook fires on every command; checks that need the previous state
cannot run at command time by construction; checks that need to know who is
acting can run only in the acting session until agents have their own
identity.
<<<
PARENT_FP: DEC-EXECUTABLE-RULES-ROADMAP:0000000
NOTES: >>>
A flow's tests become its contract once its mechanics settle, per
MECH-TESTS-AS-CONTRACT.
<<<
